course: dictionary
lesson: 8137
title: Reportedly: passing on a claim
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some sentences say that a claim comes from what people say or from news reports. «Billie Eilish and her brother Finneas reportedly recorded much of her debut album on a computer in a small bedroom.» Reportedly means according to what people say or what reports say. The writer is passing on a claim, not stating a proven fact.
explain: Reportedly is a signal word, and it tells you to be careful. In news and citizenship lessons, a careful reader checks other sources before believing a claim that uses reportedly. «Newer tools reportedly make fewer such mistakes» means someone has reported this, so the source is worth checking.
explain: In history writing, reportedly can show that a story may be a legend. «The king reportedly burnt the cakes» tells you the story is passed on, not proven. Similar words are allegedly, apparently, supposedly and it is said that. To check your meaning, swap in one of these words: if the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Reportedly means according to what people say or what news reports say.
fact: Use it when we are not completely sure that a claim is true.
fact: «A reward for a living snake over about 9 metres was reportedly never claimed.» The writer is passing on what reports say.
fact: Similar words: allegedly, apparently, supposedly, it is said that.
fact: A careful reader checks other sources before believing a claim marked with reportedly.
exercise: «Billie Eilish and her brother Finneas reportedly recorded much of her debut album at home.» What does reportedly tell the reader? | *The writer is passing on what people say, and is not completely sure it is true | The album was recorded in a studio | The writer is stating a fully proven fact | Billie Eilish did not make any music
exercise: «The king reportedly burnt the cakes.» Which word could replace reportedly and keep the same meaning? | Certainly | Never | Yesterday | *Apparently
exercise: Why should a careful reader check other sources when a claim uses reportedly? | Because sources are always written in capital letters | Because reportedly always means the claim is a lie | *Because the claim comes from reports and may not be true | Because reportedly means the writer is completely sure
test: «Newer AI tools reportedly make fewer mistakes with fingers.» What is the writer doing with this claim? | *Passing on what reports say, without proving it | Making up a new word | Proving it with a measured test | Saying the claim is false
test: Which sentence uses a word with the same meaning as reportedly? | I saw the bridge being built in one year | The bridge was built in one year, every single time we measured it | It is said that the bridge was built in one year | *It is said that the bridge was built in one year, so the story is passed on
exam: «No snake anywhere near 15 metres long has ever been measured; a reward for a living snake longer than about 9 metres was reportedly never claimed.» What is the best meaning of reportedly here? | Measured very carefully by scientists | Certainly and forever | Shouted loudly across the room | *According to reports, the reward was never claimed
